#HELP

	The available commands are:

	  ABort     ADd       Create    DElete    DIsable   ENable
	  EXit      Help      Lock      MODify    MOVe      Purge
	  Remove    SEt       SHOw      SHUtdown  STArt     STOp
	  Unlock

	To obtain more information about a command, type:

	  Help <command-name>

	Each command keyword will always be shown as a sequence of upper
	and lowercase letters.  The uppercase prefix at the beginning of
	each keyword shows the smallest abbreviation acceptable for the
	command keyword.

	Lengthy commands can be broken across line boundaries by placing
	a backslash character: "\" at the end of a partial command line.
	For example:

	  help st\
	  op

	is equivalent to the command:

	  help stop

#HELP_ABOQUE

	The command:

	  ABort Queue <queue-name> [ <grace-period> ]

	causes a SIGTERM signal to be sent to each process of each request
	presently running in the named queue.

	SIGKILL signal to all of the remaining processes for each request
	presently running in the named queue that was running when the
	ABort Queue command was first received.

	If <grace-period> is not specified, then a default value of
	twenty (20) seconds is used.

	All requests aborted by this command are lost forever.

	Operator privileges are required to use this command.

#HELP_ADD

	The commands:

	  ADd DEStination <parameters>
	  ADd DEVice <parameters>
	  ADd Form <parameters>
	  ADd Groups <parameters>
	  ADd Manager <parameters>
	  ADd Queue <parameters>
	  ADd Users <parameters>

	are used to respectively add destination(s) to an existing pipe
	queue destination set, add device(s) to an existing device queue
	device set, add a form to the device forms list, add group(s) to
	the access list for a queue, add an account to the NQS manager
	access list, add queue(s) to a queue complex, or add user(s) to
	the access list for a queue.

	More information is available:

	  Help ADd DEStination
	  Help ADd DEVice
	  Help ADd Form
	  Help ADd Groups
	  Help ADd Manager
	  Help ADd Queues
	  Help ADd Users

#HELP_ADDDES

	The commands:

	  ADd DEStination = <dest-name> <queue-name>
	  ADd DEStination = (<dest-name> [,<dest-name>...]) <queue-name>

	are used to respectively add a single destination or several
	destinations to the existing destination set for the named local
	pipe queue.

	All machine-names appearing in any queue destination names must
	be defined in the local system's network host table.

	The syntax of a pipe queue destination (<dest-name>) must conform
	to one of the following four forms:

	  local_queue_name
	  local_queue_name@local_machine_name
	  remote_queue_name@remote_machine_name
	  remote_queue_name@[remote_machine_mid]

	where the <local_machine_name> or <remote_machine_name> must be
	defined in the network host table of the local system.

	The destination syntax form:

	  remote_queue_name@[remote_machine_mid]

	allows for the explicit specification of the destination machine
	by its machine-id, entered as an integer:

	  example_queue_name@[123]

	Full NQS manager privileges are required to use this command.

#HELP_ADDDEV

	The commands:

	  ADd DEVice = <device-name> <queue-name>
	  ADd DEVice = (<device-name> [,<device-name>...]) <queue-name>

	are used to respectively add a single device, or several devices to
	the existing device set available to the named queue.  All of the
	specified devices must have been previously defined.

	The <device-name> is not specified as the fully qualified device
	pathname, but rather as the NQS "shorthand" name assigned to the
	device by the Create Device command.

	Full NQS manager privileges are required to use this command.

#HELP_ADDFOR

	The command:

	  ADd Forms <form-name> ...

	is used to add form(s) to the device forms list.

	A <form-name> can contain any printable non-blank character with
	the exception that a <form-name> cannot start with a decimal
	digit ['0'..'9'], and it cannot contain any of the Qmgr command
	punctuation characters of [',', '=', '(', ')'].

	Full NQS manager privileges are required to use this command.

#HELP_ADDGID

	The commands:
	
	  ADd Groups = <group-name> <queue-name>
	  ADd Groups = (<group-name> [,<group-name>...]) <queue-name>

	are used to add an entire group or several entire groups to
	the existing list of those that are allowed access to the
	named queue.  It is not permitted to add a group when queue
	access is unrestricted.

	A <group-name> can be specified by using either of the following
	two formats:	1. group name	2. [group-id]

	For example, if there is a group "archers" on your machine with
	group-id 10001, you may specify it with "archers" or "[10001]".

	In order for a user to have access to a queue, it is only
	necessary for ONE of the following to be true:
	
	1) Queue access is unlimited. (Help SEt Unrestricted_access)
	2) His or her login group has access.
	3) His or her account has access. (Help ADd Users)

	Full NQS manager privileges are required to use this command.

#HELP_ADDMAN

	The command:

	  ADd Managers <account-name>:{m,o} ...

	is used to add account(s) to the NQS manager access list.

	An NQS manager <account-name> can be specified by using any of
	the four following formats:

		1. local_account_name
		2. [local_user_id]
		3. [remote_user_id]@remote_machine_name
		4. [remote_user_id]@[remote_machine_mid]

	Each of the following four examples demonstrates the use of the
	above four parse constructs for an NQS manager <account-name>.

		1. kingsbur		; Local account of "kingsbur"
		2. [149]		; Local account (user-id = 149)
		3. [149]@Hal9000	; Remote account (user-id = 149)
					; on remote machine: "Hal9000"
		4. [149]@[9000]		; Remote account (user-id = 149)
					; on remote machine with machine-
					; id = 9000.

	A ":m" or ":o" suffix at the end of an <account-name> specified
	for this command is REQUIRED, and indicates the NQS Qmgr privileges
	to be assigned to the corresponding account.

	The appearance of a ":m" suffix at the end of an <account-name>
	indicates that the account should be granted full NQS manager
	privileges.  The owner of such an account will be able to use all
	of the NQS Qmgr commands.

	The appearance of a ":o" suffix at the end of an <account-name>
	indicates that the account will only be granted NQS operator
	privileges.  Such an account will have the privilege to execute
	many Qmgr commands, but will NOT have the privilege to create new
	queues, devices, forms, managers, or to delete any of the same.

	Full NQS manager privileges are required to use this command.

#HELP_ADDQUE

	The command:

	  ADd Queue = <queue-name> <complex-name>
	  ADd Queue = (<queue-name> [,<queue-name>...]) <complex-name>

	is used to add one or more queues to a queue complex.

	Full NQS manager privileges are required to use this command.

#HELP_ADDUID

	The commands:
	
	  ADd Users = <user-name> <queue-name>
	  ADd Users = (<user-name> [,<user-name>...]) <queue-name>

	are used to add a single user or several users to the existing
	list of those that are allowed access to the named queue.  It
	is not permitted to add a user when queue access is unrestricted.

	A <user-name> can be specified by using either of the following
	two formats:	1. user name	2. [user-id]

	For example, if there is a user "robin" on your machine with
	user-id 1001, you may specify him or her with "robin" or "[1001]".

	In order for a user to have access to a queue, it is only
	necessary for ONE of the following to be true:
	
	1) Queue access is unlimited. (Help SEt Unrestricted_access)
	2) His or her login group has access. (Help ADd Groups)
	3) His or her account has access.

	Full NQS manager privileges are required to use this command.

#HELP_CRE

	The commands:

	  Create Batch_queue <queue-name>
	  Create Complex <parameters>
	  Create DEVICE <parameters>
	  Create DEVICE_queue <parameters>
	  Create Pipe_queue <parameters>

	are used to respectively create a batch queue, a device, a device
	queue, or a pipe queue.

	More information is available:

	  Help Create Batch_queue
	  Help Create Complex
	  Help Create DEVICE
	  Help Create DEVICE_queue
	  Help Create Pipe_queue

#HELP_CREBATQUE

	The command:

	  Create Batch_queue <queue-name> \
	  PRiority = <queue-priority> \
	  [ PIpeonly ] \
	  [ Run_limit = <run-limit> ]

	is used to define a new NQS batch request queue.

	The name given to the queue as the <queue-name> parameter can
	contain any printable non-blank character with the exception of
	the '@' character and Qmgr command punctuation characters of
	[',', '=', '(', ')'], and must not start with a decimal digit
	['0'..'9'].

	The priority of the queue is defined by the unsigned integer
	<queue-priority> which must be in the range [0..63].

	If the optional "PIpeonly" attribute is specified, then no user
	is allowed to submit requests directly to the batch queue.  A
	queue with the "PIpeonly" attribute can only receive requests
	submitted from another pipe queue.

	The optional "Run_limit" parameter defines the maximum number of
	requests that will be allowed to run in the queue at any given
	time.  If no "Run_limit" parameter is given, then the queue is
	assigned a default run-limit of one (1).

	The queue is created in a disabled and stopped state, and must be
	explicitly enabled and started by the:

	  ENable Queue    and
	  STArt Queue

	commands.

	Full NQS manager privileges are required to use this command.

#HELP_CRECOM

	The command:

	  Create Complex = <queue-name> <complex-name>
	  Create Complex = (<queue-name> [,<queue-name>...]) <complex-name>

	is used to create a queue complex.

	The queues given as the <queue-name> parameters must already exist,
	and can only be batch queues (see Help Create Batch_queue).

	The name given to the queue complex as the <complex-name> parameter
	can contain any printable non-blank character with the exception of
	the '@' character and Qmgr command punctuation characters of
	[',', '=', '(', ')'], and must not start with a decimal digit
	['0'..'9'].

	Full NQS manager privileges are required to use this command.

#HELP_CREDEV

	The command:

	  Create DEVICE <device-name> \
	  FOrms = <forms-name> \
	  FUllname = <fully-qualified-device-path-name> \
	  Server = (<server-command-line-and-server-arguments>)

	is used to define a new NQS device.

	The name given to the device as the <device-name> parameter can
	contain any printable non-blank character with the exception of
	the '@' character and Qmgr command punctucation characters of
	[',', '=', '(', ')'], and must not start with a decimal digit
	['0'..'9'].

	The forms loaded in the device are specified by the <forms-name>
	parameter.  If the forms parameter is irrelevant for the device,
	then the value of "anyforms" should be assigned to the forms
	parameter.

	The Fullname parameter specifies the fully qualified device path
	name for the device that is being placed under NQS control.  This
	name is only used by NQS to perform the appropriate device open
	operations when necessary.  At all other times, the device is
	always referred to by the <device-name> parameter.

	The Server parameter specifies the fully qualified pathname of
	the program that is to be used as the device server for this
	device and any relevant invocation arguments.

	The device is created in disabled mode as must be explicitly
	enabled via the ENable Device command.

	Full NQS manager privileges are required to use this command.

#HELP_CREDEVQUE

	The command:

	  Create DEVICE_queue <queue-name> \
	  PRiority = <queue-priority> \
	  [ Device = <device-name> ] \
	  [ Device = (<device-name> [,<device-name>...]) ] \
	  [ PIpeonly ]

	is used to define a new NQS device request queue.

	The name given to the queue as the <queue-name> parameter can
	contain any printable non-blank character with the exception of
	the '@' character and Qmgr command punctucation characters of
	[',', '=', '(', ')'], and must not start with a decimal digit
	['0'..'9'].

	The priority of the queue is defined by the unsigned integer
	<queue-priority> which must be in the range [0..63].

	A set of devices can be assigned to the device queue at the time
	of creation using the optional "Device=" parameter.  All
	devices named in the set must have been previously defined by the
	Create DEVICE command.

	If the optional "PIpeonly" attribute is specified, then no user
	is allowed to submit requests directly to the device queue.  A
	queue with the "PIpeonly" attribute can only receive requests
	submitted from another pipe queue.

	The queue is created in a disabled and stopped state, and must be
	explicitly enabled and started by the:

	  ENable Queue    and
	  STArt Queue

	commands.

	Full NQS manager privileges are required to use this command.

#HELP_CREPIPQUE

	The command:

	  Create Pipe_queue <queue-name> \
	  PRiority = <queue-priority> \
	  Server = (<pipe-queue-server-cmnd-line-and-server-arguments>) \
	  [ Destination = <dest-name> ] \
	  [ Destination = (<dest-name> [,<dest-name>...]) ] \
	  [ PIpeonly ] \
	  [ Run_limit = <run-limit> ]

	is used to define a new NQS pipe queue.

	The name given to the queue as the <queue-name> parameter can
	contain any printable non-blank character with the exception of
	the '@' character and Qmgr command punctucation characters of
	[',', '=', '(', ')'], and must not start with a decimal digit
	['0'..'9'].

	The priority of the queue is defined by the unsigned integer
	<queue-priority> which must be in the range [0..63].

	The name of the server which will be used to transport requests
	submitted to this queue to one of the destination queues, and the
	associated server command line arguments are specified within the
	parentheses of the: "Server=" command keyword.

	A set of destination queues can be assigned to the pipe queue
	at the time of creation using the optional "DEStination="
	parameter.

	The syntax of a pipe queue destination (<dest-name>) if specified,
	must conform to one of the following four forms:

	  local_queue_name
	  local_queue_name@local_machine_name
	  remote_queue_name@remote_machine_name
	  remote_queue_name@[remote_machine_mid]

	where the <local_machine_name> or <remote_machine_name> must be
	defined in the network host table of the local system.

	The destination syntax form:

	  remote_queue_name@[remote_machine_mid]

	allows for the explicit specification of the destination machine
	by its machine-id, entered as in integer:

	  example_queue_name@[123]

	If the optional "PIpeonly" attribute is specified, then no user
	is allowed to submit requests directly to the pipe queue.  A queue
	with the "PIpeonly" attribute can only receive requests submitted
	from another pipe queue.

	The optional "Run_limit" parameter defines the maximum number of
	requests that will be allowed to run in the queue at any given time.
	If no "Run_limit" parameter is given, then the queue is assigned
	a default run-limit of one (1).

	The queue is created in a disabled and stopped state, and must be
	explicitly enabled and started by the:

	  ENable Queue    and
	  STArt Queue

	commands.

	Full NQS manager privileges are required to use this command.

#HELP_DEL

	The commands:

	  DElete Complex <complex-name>
	  DElete DEStination <parameters>
	  DElete DEVice <parameters>
	  DElete Form <parameters>
	  DElete Groups <parameters>
	  DElete Manager <parameters>
	  DElete Queue <parameters>
	  DElete Users <parameters>

	are used to respectively delete a queue complex, delete destination(s)
	for a pipe queue, delete an NQS device or delete device(s) from a
	queue device set, delete a form from the device forms list, delete
	group(s) from a queue access list, delete an account from the NQS
	manager access list, delete an NQS queue, delete request(s) from a
	queue, or delete user(s) from a queue access list.

	More information is available:

	  Help DElete Complex
	  Help DElete DEStination
	  Help DElete DEVice
	  Help DElete Form
	  Help DElete Groups
	  Help DElete Manager
	  Help DElete Queue
	  Help DElete Users

#HELP_DELCOM

	The command:

	  DElete Complex <complex-name>

	is used to delete a queue complex.

	Full NQS manager privileges are required to use this command.

#HELP_DELDES

	The commands:

	  DElete DEStination = <dest-name> <queue-name>
	  DElete DEStination = ( <dest-name> [,<dest-name>...] ) <queue-name>

	respectively delete a single destination, or several destinations
	from the destination set of a pipe queue.

	Any requests in the named queue that are currently being transferred
	to a destination that is being deleted by this command are
	allowed to successfully complete the transfer process.

	If all destinations for a pipe queue are deleted in this manner,
	then the pipe queue is effectively stopped, though its actual
	status will remain unchanged.  Thus, the addition of a new
	destination for a pipe queue that has been effectively stopped in
	the manner described, will immediately cause the queue to start
	running again.

	The syntax of a pipe queue destination (<dest-name>) must conform
	to one of the following four forms:

	  local_queue_name
	  local_queue_name@local_machine_name
	  remote_queue_name@remote_machine_name
	  remote_queue_name@[remote_machine_mid]

	where the <local_machine_name> or <remote_machine_name> must be
	defined in the network host table of the local system.

	The destination syntax form:

	  remote_queue_name@[remote_machine_mid]

	allows for the explicit specification of the destination machine
	by its machine-id, entered as an integer:

	  example_queue_name@[123]

	Full NQS manager privileges are required to use this command.

#HELP_DELDEV

	The commands:

	  DElete DEVice <device-name>
	  DElete DEVice = <device-name> <queue-name>
	  DElete DEVice = (<device-name> [,<device-name>...] ) <queue-name>

	respectively delete a device, a single device from a device set
	owned by a queue, or several devices from a device set owned by
	a queue.

	The first command deletes an NQS device.  A device cannot be
	deleted unless the device is disabled via the DIsable Device
	command.  Similarly, the device cannot be servicing an NQS
	request if it is to be deleted.  Upon deletion, the device is
	removed from all queue device mapping sets.

	The second and third forms of the DElete DEVice command are used
	to remove a device from the device set accessible to queue.  All
	requests in the named queue running on any on the named device(s)
	are allowed to complete normally.

	If the device set for any queue becomes the empty set by the
	invocation of either of the latter two commands, then the
	corresponding device queue is effectively stopped, since no
	devices are accessible to handle requests for the affected queue.

	However, the actual queue status remains unchanged for such a
	queue, so that the first addition of an enabled device to the
	device set for a queue that was effectively stopped in the manner
	described, immediately causes the queue to start running again.

	Full NQS manager privileges are required to use this command.

#HELP_DELFOR

	The command:

	  DElete Forms <form-name> ...

	is used to delete form(s) from the device forms list.

	Full NQS manager privileges are required to use this command.

#HELP_DELGID

	The commands:
	
	  DElete Groups = <group-name> <queue-name>
	  DElete Groups = (<group-name> [,<group-name>...]) <queue-name>

	are used to delete an entire group or several entire groups
	from the existing list of those that are allowed access to the
	named queue.  This command can only delete groups that have
	been added with the ADd Groups command.  It is not permitted to
	delete a group from a queue where access is unrestricted.

	A <group-name> can be specified by using either of the following
	two formats:	1. group name	2. [group-id]

	For example, if there is a group "archers" on your machine with
	group-id 10001, you may specify it with "archers" or "[10001]".
	If the group name does not appear in the system group file,
	the latter form must be used.

	In order for a user to have access to a queue, it is only
	necessary for ONE of the following to be true:
	
	1) Queue access is unlimited. (Help SEt NO_Access)
	2) His or her login group has access.
	3) His or her account has access. (Help DElete Users)

	Full NQS manager privileges are required to use this command.

#HELP_DELMAN

	The command:

	  DElete Managers <account-name>:{m,o} ...

	is used to delete account(s) from the NQS manager access list.

	Attempts to exclude the system root account from the NQS manager
	set will be silently ignored.

	An NQS manager <account-name> can be specified by using any of
	the four following formats:

		1. local_account_name
		2. [local_user_id]
		3. [remote_user_id]@remote_machine_name
		4. [remote_user_id]@[remote_machine_mid]

	Each of the following four examples demonstrates the use of the
	above four parse constructs for an NQS manager <account-name>.

		1. kingsbur		; Local account of "kingsbur"
		2. [149]		; Local account (user-id = 149)
		3. [149]@Hal9000	; Remote account (user-id = 149)
					; on remote machine: "Hal9000"
		4. [149]@[9000]		; Remote account (user-id = 149)
					; on remote machine with machine-
					; id = 9000.

	Full NQS manager privileges are required to use this command.

#HELP_DELQUE

	The command:

	  DElete Queue <queue-name>

	deletes the named queue.  To delete a queue, no requests can be
	present in the queue and the queue must be disabled.  All of the
	queue device set mappings for the deleted queue are also deleted.

	Full NQS manager privileges are required to use this command.

#HELP_DELREQ

	The command:

	  DElete Request <request-id> ...

	is used to delete a request on the local NQS machine.

	If the specified request is running, then it is terminated by
	the method described in the ABort Queue command.

	If the request is not running, then it is simply removed from
	its containing queue and discarded.

	Operator privileges are required to use this command.

#HELP_DELUID

	The commands:
	
	  DElete Users = <user-name> <queue-name>
	  DElete Users = (<user-name> [,<user-name>...]) <queue-name>

	are used to delete a single user or several users from the
	existing list of those that are allowed access to the named
	queue.  This command can only delete users who have been
	added with the ADd Users command.  It is not permitted to
	delete a user from a queue where access is unrestricted.

	A <user-name> can be specified by using either of the following
	two formats:	1. user name	2. [user-id]

	For example, if there is a user "robin" on your machine with
	user-id 1001, you may specify him or her with "robin" or "[1001]".
	If the group name does not appear in the system group file,
	the latter form must be used.

	In order for a user to have access to a queue, it is only
	necessary for ONE of the following to be true:
	
	1) Queue access is unlimited. (Help SEt NO_Access)
	2) His or her login group has access. (Help DElete Groups)
	3) His or her account has access.

	Full NQS manager privileges are required to use this command.

#HELP_DIS

	The commands:

	  DIsable Device <parameters>
	  DIsable Queue <parameters>

	are used to respectively disable an NQS device, or NQS queue.

	More information is available:

	  Help DIsable Device
	  Help DIsable Queue

#HELP_DISDEV

	The command:

	  DIsable Device <device-name>

	disables the named device.  The current request (if any) being
	handled by the device will be allowed to complete.  After that,
	the device is prevented from handling any more requests until it
	is enabled by the ENable Device command.

	If the device disabled by this command was the last enabled device
	in a device set for a particular queue, then the queue is
	effectively stopped, since no more enabled devices appear in the
	device set for the particular queue.

	However, the actual queue status remains unchanged for such a
	queue, so that the first addition of an enabled device to the
	device set for a queue that was effectively stopped in the manner
	described, immediately causes the queue to start running again.

	Operator privileges are required to use this command.

#HELP_DISQUE

	The command:

	  DIsable Queue <queue-name>

	is used to prevent any more requests from being placed in the named
	queue.

	Operator privileges are required to use this command.

#HELP_ENA

	The commands:

	  ENable Device <parameters>
	  ENable Queue <parameters>

	are used to respectively enable an NQS device, or NQS queue.

	More information is available:

	  Help ENable Device
	  Help ENable Queue

#HELP_ENADEV

	The command:

	  ENable Device <device-name>

	is used to enable the named device.  If the device is already
	enabled, then no operation is performed.  Otherwise, the device
	becomes available to handle device queue requests.

	Operator privileges are required to use this command.

#HELP_ENAQUE

	The command:

	  ENable Queue <queue-name>

	is used to enable the named queue.  If the queue is already
	enabled, the no operation is performed.  Otherwise, the queue
	is enabled to accept new requests.

	Operator privileges are required to use this command.

#HELP_EXI

	The command:

	  EXit

	is used to exit from the NQS manager program.  The NQS manager
	program is also exited if an end-of-file is encountered on the
	standard input file.  Thus, typing ^D or EXit will cause the
	NQS manager program to exit.

#HELP_LOCDAE

	The command:
	
	  Lock Local_daemon
	  
	is used to lock the NQS local daemon into memory.
	See plock(2).
	
	Operator privileges are required to use this command.

#HELP_MODREQ

	The command:
	
	  MODify Request <request-id> \
			 [ Nice_limit = <nice-value> ] \
	  		 [ RTime_limit = <tlimit-value> ] \
	  		 [ RMemory_limit = <mlimit-value> ]
	  
	is used to modify the parameters of a request which is already
	in its final destination queue, but has not started yet.

	Several parameters may be changed.  Nice_limit is the initial nice
	value of the request, RTime_limit is the per-request time limit
	for the request, and RMemory_limit is the per-request memory limit
	for the request.
	
	Operator privileges are required to use this command.

#HELP_MOV

	The commands:
	
	  MOVe Queue <parameters>
	  MOVe Request <parameters>
	  
	are used to move requests between queues.
	
	More information is available:

	  Help MOVe Queue
	  Help MOVe Request

#HELP_MOVQUE

	The command:
	
	  MOVe Queue <from-queue-name> <to-queue-name>
	  
	is used to move all requests from the queue specified by
	<from-queue-name> to the queue specified by <to-queue-name>.

	Operator privileges are required to use this command.

#HELP_MOVREQ

	The commands:
	
	  MOVe Request = <request-id> <queue-name>
	  MOVe Request = (<request-id> [ <request-id> ... ] ) <queue-name>
	  
	respectively move one or a number requests from the queue they are
	currently in to the queue specified by <queue-name>.

	Operator privileges are required to use this command.

#HELP_PURQUE

	The command:

	  Purge Queue <queue-name>

	causes all requests that are not running to be purged (dropped)
	from the named queue.  The purged requests are irretrievably lost.

	Operator privileges are required to use this command.

#HELP_REMQUE

	The command:
	
	  Remove Queue = (<queue-name> [,<queue-name>...]) <complex-name>
	  
	is used to remove one or more queues specified by <queue-name>
	from the queue complex specified by <complex-name>.

	Full NQS manager privileges are required to use this command.

#HELP_SET

	The command:

	  SEt <parameters>

	has many possible forms.  More information is available:

	  Help SEt COMplex		Help SEt CORefile_limit
	  Help SEt DAta_limit		Help SEt DEBug
	  Help SEt DEFault		Help SEt DEStination
	  Help SEt DEVICE		Help SEt DEVICE_server
	  Help SEt Form			Help SEt Global
	  Help SEt HARDUlimit		Help SEt LIfetime
	  Help SEt NODE_Group
	  Help SEt LOg_file		Help SEt MAIl
	  Help SEt MANager		Help SEt MAXimum
	  Help SEt NEtwork		Help SEt NIce_value_limit
	  Help SEt NO_Access		Help SEt NO_Default
	  Help SEt NO_Network_daemon	Help SEt Open_wait
	  Help SEt PER_Process		Help SEt PER_Request
	  Help SEt PIpe_client		Help SEt PRiority
	  Help SEt Run_limit		Help SEt SHell_strategy
	  Help SEt SOFTUlimit
	  Help SEt STack_limit		Help SEt Unrestricted_access
	  Help SEt Working_set_limit	Help SEt Queue_request_limit

#HELP_SETCOMRUNLIM

	The command:

	  SEt COMplex Run_limit = <runlimit-value> <complex-name>

	is used to change the run-limit of an NQS queue complex.
	The queue complex specified by <complex-name> must already exist.

	The run-limit for a queue complex determines the maximum number of
	requests that will be allowed to run in all the queues in that 
	queue complex at any given time.

	Operator privileges are required to use this command.

#HELP_SETCOR

	The command:

	  SEt CORefile_limit = (<corefile-size-limit>) <batch-queue-name>

	is used to set the per-process maximum core file size limit
	(the largest size of a "core" file that can be created for
	a process) for any batch request subsequently placed in the
	named batch queue, when the request does not otherwise define
	an explicit per-process maximum core file size limit (as chosen
	by the originating user).

	Each batch request when placed in a batch queue, is examined to
	ensure that any user-specified core file size limit for the
	request does not exceed the core file size limit (if configured),
	for the containing batch queue.

	If an attempt is made to queue a batch request which asks for a
	larger core file size limit than the destination batch queue has
	defined as allowable, then the request is rejected.

	The maximum core file size limit assigned to a particular batch
	request is determined at the time that the request is queued.
	If the core file size limit for the containing batch queue is
	later reduced, no requests in the batch queue will be affected.
	However, if the new core file size limit value is less than the
	core file size limit requested by a previously queued request,
	then a warning diagnostic is displayed.

	The core file size of ANY process comprising the running batch
	request cannot exceed the maximum in force when the request
	was queued.

	A <corefile-size-limit> is specified as a single integer less
	than 100,000,000 with an optional fractional part, possibly
	followed by one of the case insensitive unit names of:

		Unit name:	Meaning:

		 b		 bytes
		 w		 words
		 kb		 2 to the 10th bytes
		 kw		 2 to the 10th words
		 mb		 2 to the 20th bytes
		 mw		 2 to the 20th words
		 gb		 2 to the 30th bytes
		 gw		 2 to the 30th words


	Example valid <corefile-size-limit> specifications are therefore:

		200000		(specifies 200,000 bytes)
		200000B		(specifies 200,000 bytes)
		200 Kw		(specifies 204,800 words)
		.2 MW		(specifies 209,715 words)

	Internally, qmgr converts the specified value into bytes
	or words, whichever is more natural for your machine.
	If the resulting value cannot be represented in a signed
	long integer, then qmgr gives you the largest value that
	it can.
	
	Depending on the underlying UNIX implementation upon which NQS
	is running, this limit may, or may not be configurable.  If
	core file size limits are not supported by the host UNIX
	operating system, then an appropriate error diagnostic is
	displayed, upon the use of this command.

	Full NQS manager privileges are required to use this command.

#HELP_SETDAT

	The command:

	  SEt DAta_limit = (<data-limit>) <batch-queue-name>

	is used to set the per-process maximum data-segment size limit
	for any batch request subsequently placed in the named batch
	queue, when the batch request does not otherwise define an
	explicit per-process maximum data-segment size limit (as chosen
	by the originating user).

	Each batch request, when queued, is examined to ensure that any
	user-specified data-segment size limit for the request does not
	exceed the data-segment size limit (if configured), for the
	containing batch queue.

	If an attempt is made to queue a batch request which asks for a
	larger data-segment size limit than the destination batch queue
	has defined as allowable, then the request is rejected.

	The maximum data-segment size limit assigned to a particular
	batch request is determined at the time that the request is
	queued.  If the data-segment size limit for the containing batch
	queue is later reduced, no requests in the queue will be affected.
	However, if the new data-segment size limit value is less than
	the data-segment size limit requested by a previously queued
	request, then a warning diagnostic is displayed.

	The data-segment size of ANY process comprising the running batch
	request cannot exceed the maximum in force when the request was
	queued.

	A <data-limit> is specified as a single integer less than
	100,000,000 with an optional fractional part, possibly followed
	by one of the case insensitive unit names of:

		Unit name:	Meaning:

		 b		 bytes
		 w		 words
		 kb		 2 to the 10th bytes
		 kw		 2 to the 10th words
		 mb		 2 to the 20th bytes
		 mw		 2 to the 20th words
		 gb		 2 to the 30th bytes
		 gw		 2 to the 30th words


	Example valid <data-limit> specifications are therefore:

		200000		(specifies 200,000 bytes)
		200000B		(specifies 200,000 bytes)
		200 Kw		(specifies 204,800 words)
		.2 MW		(specifies 209,715 words)

	Internally, qmgr converts the specified value into bytes
	or words, whichever is more natural for your machine.
	If the resulting value cannot be represented in a signed
	long integer, then qmgr gives you the largest value that
	it can.
	
	Depending on the underlying UNIX implementation upon which NQS
	is running, this limit may, or may not be configurable.  If
	data-segment size limits are not supported by the host UNIX
	operating system, then an appropriate error diagnostic is
	displayed, upon the use of this command.

	Full NQS manager privileges are required to use this command.

#HELP_SETDEB

	The command:

	  SEt DEBug <integer-debug-level>

	allows an NQS manager to enable or disable NQS debugging output.
	Currently, only 4 levels of debugging are supported, namely no
	debugging or debugging enabled.  A debug value of 0 turns off
	debug information, while a non-zero value enables debugging.
	A debug value of 0 means only catastrophic failures are reported in the
        NQS log; the maximum debug level of 3 means even trivial errors
        are reported, thus greatly increasing the amount of disk space used.

	Full NQS manager privileges are required to use this command.

#HELP_SETDEF

	The commands:

	  SEt DEFault Batch_request <parameters>
	  SEt DEFault DEStination_retry <parameters>
	  SEt DEFault DEVice_request <parameters>
	  SEt DEFault Print_request <parameters>

	are used to set default batch request, default destination,
	default device request, and default print request characteristics.

	More information is available:

	  Help SEt DEFault Batch_request
	  Help SEt DEFault DEStination_retry
	  Help SEt DEFault DEVice_request
	  Help SEt DEFault Print_request

#HELP_SETDEFBAT

	The commands:

	  SEt DEFault Batch_request Priority <parameters>
	  SEt DEFault Batch_request Queue <parameters>

	are used to respectively set the default queue to be used for
	batch request submission, and the default batch request intra-
	queue priority.

	More information is available:

	  Help SEt DEFault Batch_request Priority
	  Help SEt DEFault Batch_request Queue

#HELP_SETDEFBATPRI

	The command:

	  SEt DEFault Batch_request Priority <integer-priority>

	is used to set the intra-queue priority for batch requests.

	If a batch request is submitted without a specified intra-queue
	priority, then a default intra-queue priority is assigned to the
	request.  This default priority is set by this command.

	The priority selected is termed the "intra-queue priority"
	because the selected priority determines the relative ordering
	of requests within the selected batch queue--not the execution-time
	priority of the request.  This integer priority value must be in the
	interval [0..63].

	Full NQS manager privileges are required to use this command.

#HELP_SETDEFBATQUE

	The command:

	  SEt DEFault Batch_request Queue <queue-name> 

	is used to set the default queue to be used for batch requests in
	the absence of an explicit queue specification.

	Full NQS manager privileges are required to use this command.

#HELP_SETDEFDES

	The commands:

	  SEt DEFault DEStination_retry Time <parameters>
	  SEt DEFault DEStination_retry Wait <parameters>

	are used to respectively set the default unreachable destination
	retry tolerance and the default unreachable destination retry wait
	time.

	More information is available:

	  Help SEt DEFault DEStination_retry Time
	  Help SEt DEFault DEStination_retry Wait

#HELP_SETDEFDESTIM

	The command:

	  SEt DEFault DEStination_retry Time <elapsed-retry-time-in-hours>

	is used to set the default number of hours that can elapse during
	which time a pipe queue destination can be unreachable, before
	being marked as completely failed.

	See also the description of the:

	  SEt DEFault DEStination_retry Wait

	command for more information.

	Full NQS manager privileges are required to use this command.

#HELP_SETDEFDESWAI

	The command:

	  SEt DEFault DEStination_retry Wait <retry-wait-time-in-minutes>

	is used to set the default number of minutes to wait before
	retrying a pipe queue destination that was unreachable at the
	time of the last attempt.

	When a pipe queue destination fails to accept a request because
	of a network failure, or remote server failure, then the request
	is not transferred, and the destination is disabled for the number
	of minutes as determined by the <retry-wait-time-in-minutes>
	parameter.  At the end of this time, the destination is re-enabled,
	and retried as appropriate.

	To prevent an infinite number of retries, an additional command
	exists:

	  SEt DEFault DEStination_retry Time <parameter>

	which prevents a pipe queue destination from being endlessly
	retried forever.

	Full NQS manager privileges are required to use this command.

#HELP_SETDEFDEVPRI

	The command:

	  SEt DEFault DEVice_request Priority <integer-priority>

	is used to set the default intra-queue priority for device requests.

	If a device request is submitted without a specified intra-queue
	priority, then a default intra-queue priority is assigned to the
	request.  This default priority is set by this command.

	The priority selected is termed the "intra-queue priority"
	because the selected priority determines the relative ordering
	of requests within the selected device queue--not the execution-time
	priority of the request.  This integer priority value must be in the
	interval [0..63].

	Full NQS manager privileges are required to use this command.

#HELP_SETDEFPRI

	The commands:

	  SEt DEFault Print_request Forms <parameters>
	  SEt DEFault Print_request Queue <parameters>

	are used to respectively set the default forms and default queue
	to be used for print request submission.

	More information is available:

	  Help SEt DEFault Print_request Forms
	  Help SEt DEFault Print_request Queue

#HELP_SETDEFPRIFOR

	The command:

	  SEt DEFault Print_request Forms <default-print-forms-name>

	is used to set def default print forms assigned to a print request
	in the absence of an explicit forms specification.  The default
	value for the default forms parameter is "anyforms."

	Full NQS manager privileges are required to use this command.

#HELP_SETDEFPRIQUE

	The command:

	  SEt DEFault Print_request Queue <queue-name>

	is used to set the default queue to be used for print request in
	the absence of an explicit queue specification.

	Full NQS manager privileges are required to use this command.

#HELP_SETDES

	The commands:

	  SEt DEStination = <dest-name> <queue-name>
	  SEt DEStination = (<dest-name> [,<dest-name>...]) <queue-name>

	are used to respectively set the destination set for the named
	pipe queue to a single destination, or several destinations.
	All previous destinations in the pipe queue destination set are
	discarded.

	All machine-names appearing in any queue destination names must
	be defined in the local system's network host table.

	The syntax of a pipe queue destination (<dest-name>) must conform
	to one of the following four forms:

	  local_queue_name
	  local_queue_name@local_machine_name
	  remote_queue_name@remote_machine_name
	  remote_queue_name@[remote_machine_mid]

	where the <local_machine_name> or <remote_machine_name> must be
	defined in the network host table of the local system.

	The destination syntax form:

	  remote_queue_name@[remote_machine_mid]

	allows for the explicit specification of the destination machine
	by its machine-id, entered as an integer:

	  example_queue_name@[123]

	Full NQS manager privileges are required to use this command.

#HELP_SETDEV

	The commands:

	  SEt DEVICE = <device-name> <queue-name>
	  SEt DEVICE = (<device-name> [,<device-name>...]) <queue-name>

	are used to set the device set for the named device queue to a
	single device, or several devices.

	Use of the SEt DEVICE command causes all previous devices in the
	device set for the named queue to be discarded.  The device set
	for the named queue is then set to include only the specified
	devices.

	Full NQS manager privileges are required to use this command.

#HELP_SETDEVSER

	The command:

	  SEt DEVICE_server = (<server-name-and-arguments>) <device-name>

	is used to set the server and related server arguments used by the
	named device.

	The text within the enclosing parentheses defines the server
	command line that will be used to execute requests handled by the
	named device.

	Full NQS manager privileges are required to use this command.

#HELP_SETFOR

	The commands:

	  SEt Forms <forms-name> ...
	  SEt Forms = <forms-name> <device-name>

	respectively set the device forms list to the specified form(s),
	and set the forms for the named device.

	A <form-name> can contain any printable non-blank character with
	the exception that a <form-name> cannot start with a decimal
	digit ['0'..'9'], and it cannot contain any of the Qmgr command
	punctuation characters of [',', '=', '(', ')'].

	Full NQS manager privileges are required for the use of the

	  SEt Forms <forms-name> ...

	command, while operator privileges will suffice for successful
	execution of the:

	  SEt Forms = <forms-name> <device-name>

	command.


#HELP_SETGBLBATLIM

	The command:

	  SEt Global Batch_limit = <newlimit>

	is used to set the maximum number of batch requests that can RUN
	in the local host simultaneously.

	The value can be confirmed in the "SHOw Parameters" display.

	Operator privileges are required to use this command.


#HELP_SETLIFTIM

	The command:

	  SEt LIfetime <pipe-queue-request-lifetime-in-hours>

	is used to set the lifetime of requests queued in pipe queues.

	This value determines the number of hours that any request can
	reside within a pipe queue, from the time at which the request
	was first placed within the queue.  If any request resides within
	a pipe queue for a duration of time equal to or greater than this
	limit (for whatever reason: i.e. the pipe queue is stopped), then
	the request is deleted, and mail is sent informing the owner of
	the request that the request has been deleted.

	The "LIfetime" parameter exists to prevent requests from filling
	up all available queue space because of network failures, or other
	causes that would prevent requests from being delivered to their
	respective destinations.

	This value should be typically configured so as to describe a
	fairly long time interval (i.e. 72 hours).

	Alternatively, the "LIfetime" parameter can be assigned a value
	of 0, which indicates that all requests residing within a pipe
	queue have an infinite lifetime.

	Full NQS manager privileges are required to use this command.

#HELP_SETLOGFIL

	The command:

	  SEt LOg_file <logfile-name>

	is used to change the file being used for NQS error logging.  If
	the specified file is successfully opened for append, then NQS
	writes a message on the old logfile stating that a switch is being
	made to the new logfile.

	Otherwise, an error message is written on the current log file and
	the current logfile is not changed.

	Full NQS manager privileges are required to use this command.

#HELP_SETMAI

	The command:

	  SEt MAIl <account-name>

	is used to set the account name that will appear in the "From:"
	portion of mail sent by NQS to notify users (when appropriate), of
	certain events concerning their NQS request(s).

	The mail <account-name> can contain any printable non-blank
	character with the exception that an <account-name> cannot start
	with a decimal digit ['0'..'9'], and it cannot contain any of the
	Qmgr command punctuation characters of [',', '=', '(', ')'].

	Full NQS manager privileges are required to use this command.

#HELP_SETMAN

	The command:

	  SEt MANagers <account-name>:{m,o} ...

	is used to set the NQS manager access list to the specified
	account(s).

	An NQS manager <account-name> can be specified by using any of
	the four following formats:

		1. local_account_name
		2. [local_user_id]
		3. [remote_user_id]@remote_machine_name
		4. [remote_user_id]@[remote_machine_mid]

	Each of the following four examples demonstrates the use of the
	above four parse constructs for an NQS manager <account-name>.

		1. kingsbur		; Local account of "kingsbur"
		2. [149]		; Local account (user-id = 149)
		3. [149]@Hal9000	; Remote account (user-id = 149)
					; on remote machine: "Hal9000"
		4. [149]@[9000]		; Remote account (user-id = 149)
					; on remote machine with machine-
					; id = 9000.

	A ":m" or ":o" suffix at the end of an <account-name> specified
	for this command is REQUIRED, and indicates the NQS Qmgr privileges
	to be assigned to the corresponding account.

	The appearance of a ":m" suffix at the end of an <account-name>
	indicates that the account should be granted full NQS manager
	privileges.  The owner of such an account will be able to use all
	of the NQS Qmgr commands.

	The appearance of a ":o" suffix at the end of an <account-name>
	indicates that the account will only be granted NQS operator
	privileges.  Such an account will have the privilege to execute
	many Qmgr commands, but will NOT have the privilege to create new
	queues, devices, forms, managers, or to delete any of the same.

	Attempts to exclude the system root account from the NQS manager
	set as a fully privileged manager account (privilege=m), will be
	silently ignored.

	Full NQS manager privileges are required to use this command.

#HELP_SETSOFTULIM

	The command:

	  SEt SOFTUlimit=<number-of-nodes>

	is used to set the soft user_limit. The soft user_limit specifies
	the total number of nodes a single user may use (in RUN state) at
	a time. However, this limit can be exceeded if the batch partition
	is not fully utilized and there are no other requests pending.

	A user can submit as many jobs as he/she wishes, but the number of 
	nodes that can be in RUN state by a user will be limited by the 
	soft limits unless the batch partition is not fully utilized. 
	The rest of the unscheduled jobs will be waiting in the queues.
 

	Full NQS manager privileges are required to use this command.

#HELP_SETHARDULIM

	The command:

	  SEt HARDUlimit=<number-of-nodes>

	is used to set the hard user_limit. The hard user_limit specifies
	the absolute maximum number of nodes a single user may use 
	(in RUN state) at a time. This limit cannot be exceeded even if 
	the batch partition is not fully utilized.

	A user can submit as many jobs as he/she wishes, but the number of 
	nodes that can be in RUN state by a user will be limited by the 
	hard limits. The rest of the unscheduled jobs will be waiting in 
	the queues.
 

	Full NQS manager privileges are required to use this command.

#HELP_SETMAX

	The commands:

	  SEt MAXimum Copies <parameters>
	  SEt MAXimum Open_retries <parameters>
	  SEt MAXimum Print_size <parameters>

	are used to respectively set the maximum number of copies that
	may be generated by a device request, the maximum number of failed
	device open retries, and the maximum size of a print file request
	accepted by NQS.

	More information is available:

	  Help SEt MAXimum Copies
	  Help SEt MAXimum Open_retries
	  Help SEt MAXimum Print_size

#HELP_SETMAXCOP

	The command:

	  SEt MAXimum Copies <integer-max-print-copies>

	is used to set the maximum number of copies of a file that can be
	printed by an NQS print request.  NQS will refuse to print more than
	this many copies of a print file.

	Full NQS manager privileges are required to use this command.

#HELP_SETMAXOPERET

	The command:

	  SEt MAXimum Open_retries <integer-retry-count>

	is used to set the maximum number of times that NQS will try to
	open a device prior to spawning a device server to handle an NQS
	device request.  If an error occurs trying to open a device, then
	this limit prevents NQS from trying open a failed device forever.

	If this limit is exceeded trying to open a device, then the
	device is marked as failed.

	Full NQS manager privileges are required to use this command.

#HELP_SETMAXPRISIZ

	The command:

	  SEt MAXimum Print_size <integer-max-print-file-size>

	is used to set the maximum size of an NQS print file in bytes.
	NQS will refuse to print files larger than this size.

	Full NQS manager privileges are required to use this command.

#HELP_SETNDF

	The commands:

	  SEt NO_Default Batch_request Queue
	  SEt NO_Default Print_request Forms
	  SEt NO_Default Print_request Queue

	are used to respectively define that no default batch request
	queue exists, no default print request forms exist, and that no
	default print request queue exists.

	More information is available:

	  Help SEt NO_Default Batch_request Queue
	  Help SEt NO_Default Print_request Forms
	  Help SEt NO_Default Print_request Queue

#HELP_SETNDFBATQUE

	The command:

	  SEt NO_Default Batch_request Queue

	is used to indicate that no default batch request queue is to
	be defined.

	Full NQS manager privileges are required to use this command.

#HELP_SETNDFPRI

	The commands:

	  SEt NO_Default Print_request Forms
	  SEt NO_Default Print_request Queue

	are used to respectively define that no default print request
	forms exist, and that no default print request queue exists.

	More information is available:

	  Help SEt NO_Default Print_request Forms
	  Help SEt NO_Default Print_request Queue

#HELP_SETNDFPRIFOR

	The command:

	  SEt NO_Default Print_request Forms

	is used to indicate that no default print request forms are
	defined for the local NQS system.  Print requests executed
	on the local system that do not explicitly define any required
	forms, will run on the first available device for the device
	queue in which they are entered.

	Full NQS manager privileges are required to use this command.

#HELP_SETNDFPRIQUE

	The command:

	  SEt NO_Default Print_request Queue

	is used to indicate that no default print request queue is to
	be defined.

	Full NQS manager privileges are required to use this command.

#HELP_SETNET

	The commands:

	  SEt NEtwork Client <parameters>
	  SEt NEtwork Daemon <parameters>
	  SEt NEtwork Server <parameters>

	are used to respectively define the network client (server
	for all local network queues), the network daemon, and the
	network server.

	More information is available:

	  Help SEt NEtwork Client
	  Help SEt NEtwork Daemon
	  Help SEt NEtwork Server

#HELP_SETNETCLI

	The command:
	
	  SEt NEtwork Client = (<client-name-and-arguments>)

	is used to set and configure the network client and the
	arguments thereto. The network client stages out batch
	request output files and empties network queues.

	The text within the enclosing parentheses defines the
	command line that will be used to execute the client.

	Full NQS manager privileges are required to use this command.

#HELP_SETNETDAE

	The command:
	
	  SEt NEtwork Daemon = (<daemon-name-and-arguments>)

	is used to set and configure the network daemon and the
	arguments thereto. The network daemon listens for messages
	from remote clients.

	The text within the enclosing parentheses defines the
	command line that will be used to execute the daemon.

	Full NQS manager privileges are required to use this command.

#HELP_SETNETSER

	The command:
	
	  SEt NEtwork Server = (<server-name-and-arguments>)

	is used to set and configure the network server and the
	arguments thereto. The network server processes submission
	requests from remote pipe clients.

	The text within the enclosing parentheses defines the
	command line that will be used to execute the server.

	Full NQS manager privileges are required to use this command.

#HELP_SETNIC

	The command:

	  SEt NIce_value_limit = <nice-value> <batch-queue-name>

	is used to set the per-process UNIX nice-value limit, for any
	batch request subsequently placed in the named batch queue,
	when the request does not otherwise define an explicit per-
	rocess nice-value (as chosen by the originating user).

	Each batch request, when queued, is examined to ensure that any
	user-specified nice-value for the request does not exceed the
	nice-value (if configured), for the containing batch queue.
	That is, the user-specified nice-value cannot be NUMERICALLY
	LESS than the nice-value as configured for the containing batch
	queue.

	If an attempt is made to queue a batch request which asks for a
	nice-value which would assign all processes in the request an
	execution priority higher than the nice-value chosen by the
	destination batch queue (request specifies nice-value NUMERICALLY
	LESS than the nice-value defined for the batch queue), then the
	request is rejected.

	It must be remembered that nice-values in the range [-20..-1]
	actually specify a HIGHER execution priority--than the default
	of 0, while values in the range [1..19] ([1..20] on Berkeley
	systems), specify a LOWER execution priority than the default
	value.

	The nice-value assigned to a particular batch request is deter-
	mined at the time that the request is queued.  If the nice-value
	limit for the batch queue is later raised (i.e. the new nice-
	alue is numerically greater than the old nice-value), no requests
	in the queue will be affected.  However, if the new nice-value
	limit is numerically greater than the nice-value requested by a
	previously queued request, then a warning diagnostic is displayed.

	Unless a process of the request is running with an effective
	user-id of root, it will be unable to lower its nice value
	(raise its execution priority) from the value determined
	when the request was queued.

	A <nice-value> is always specified as an integer in the range
	[-20..20].  On System V based UNIX implementations, a specifi-
	cation of "20" is silently interpreted as "19", since only
	Berkeley UNIX implementations are capable of using the highest
	value of "20".

	Full NQS manager privileges are required to use this command.

#HELP_SETNODEGRP

	The command:

	  SEt NODE_Group = <node_group-value> <batch-queue-name>

	is used to set the scheduling node_group value, for any
	batch request subsequently placed in the named batch queue.


	A <node_group-value> is always specified as an integer in the range
	[0..63].

	Full NQS manager privileges are required to use this command.

#HELP_SETNNEDAE

	The command:
	
	  SEt NO_Network_daemon
	  
	is used to indicate that no network daemon is to be defined.
	
	Full NQS manager privileges are required to use this command.
	
#HELP_SETNOACC

	The command:

	  SEt NO_Access <queue-name>

	is used to delete all groups and all users from the access list
	for the named queue.  Requests in the queue will be allowed
	to remain there.  In order to enforce access restrictions on
	a queue where access is currently unrestricted, start with
	this command and then add groups or users as needed.
	
	Root always has access to all enabled queues, even in the
	absence of the appropriate entries in the access list.
	If you wish to deny access to root, see DIsable Queue.

	In order for a user to have access to a queue, it is only
	necessary for ONE of the following to be true:
	
	1) Queue access is unlimited.
	2) His or her login group has access. (Help DElete Groups)
	3) His or her account has access. (Help DElete Users)

	Full NQS manager privileges are required to use this command.

#HELP_SETOPEWAI

	The command:

	  SEt Open_wait <open-wait-seconds>

	is used to set the number of seconds between failed device
	open attempts that NQS will wait before trying to open the
	device again.

	Full NQS manager privileges are required to use this command.

#HELP_SETPERPRO

	The commands:

	  SEt PER_Process Cpu_limit <parameters>
	  SEt PER_Process Memory_limit <parameters>
	  SEt PER_Process Permfile_limit <parameters>
	  SEt PER_Process Tempfile_limit <parameters>

	are used to set the PER-PROCESS maximum CPU-time limit, maximum
	memory consumption limit, maximum permanent-file size limit,
	and maximum temporary-file size limit, for all batch requests
	subsequently placed in the named batch queue, in the absence of
	an explicit corresponding user-specified quota limit.

	Each batch request when placed in a batch queue, is examined
	to ensure that all of the user-specified quota limits for the
	request do not exceed the corresponding maximum limit for the
	containing batch queue (provided that the corresponding queue
	limit has been configured).

	If an attempt is made to queue a batch request which asks for
	more of a particular resource than the destination batch queue
	has defined as allowable, then the request is rejected.

	All possible limits that can be applied to a particular batch
	request are resolved at the time that the request is queued.
	If a particular batch queue limit is later reduced, no requests
	in the queue will be affected.  However, if the new limit value
	is less than the corresponding limit requested by a previously
	queued batch request, then a warning diagnostic is displayed.

	Depending on the underlying UNIX implementation upon which NQS
	is running, some, all, or none of these limits may be configurable.
	If the named quota limit is not supported by the underlying
	operating system, then an appropriate error diagnostic is
	displayed, if an attempt is made to set the unsupported limit.

	More information is available:

	  Help SEt PER_Process Cpu_limit
	  Help SEt PER_Process Memory_limit
	  Help SEt PER_Process Permfile_limit
	  Help SEt PER_Process Tempfile_limit

	Full NQS manager privileges are required to use this command.

#HELP_SETPERPROCPUT

	The command:

	  SEt PER_Process Cpu_limit = (<cpu-time-limit>) <batch-queue-name>

	is used to set the PER-PROCESS maximum CPU-time limit for any
	batch request subsequently placed in the named batch queue, when
	the request does not otherwise define an explicit PER-PROCESS
	maximum CPU-time limit (as chosen by the originating user).

	Each batch request when placed in a batch queue, is examined to
	ensure that any user-specified CPU-time limit for the request
	does not exceed the CPU-time limit (if configured), for the
	containing batch queue.

	If an attempt is made to queue a batch request which asks for
	more CPU-time than the destination batch queue has defined as
	allowable, then the request is rejected.

	The maximum CPU-time to be given to a particular batch request
	at execution time is determined at the time that the request is
	queued.  If the CPU-time limit for the containing batch queue
 	is later reduced, no requests in the queue will be affected.
	However, if the new CPU-time limit value is less than the
	CPU-time limit requested by a previously queued request, then
	a warning diagnostic is displayed.

	The CPU-time consumed by ANY process comprising the running
	batch request cannot exceed the PER-PROCESS maximum determined
	when the request was queued.

	A <cpu-time-limit> may be specified as seconds, minutes and
	seconds, or hours, minutes, and seconds. Milliseconds may
	be included at the setter's option. Milliseconds may also
	appear alone. Here are the accepted formats:

		hh:mm:ss.ms	hh:mm:ss
		mm:ss.ms	mm:ss
		ss.ms		ss
		.ms
	
	Example valid <cpu-time-limit> specifications are therefore:

		2010		(specifies 2,010 seconds)
		2010.234	(specifies 2,010 seconds and 234
				 milliseconds)
		59:23.21	(specifies 59 minutes, 23 seconds,
				 and 210 milliseconds)
		99912:0:0	(specifies 99,912 hours, 0 minutes,
				 0 seconds, and 0 milliseconds)

	Depending on the underlying UNIX implementation upon which NQS
	is running, this limit may, or may not be configurable.  If
	CPU-time limits are not supported by the host UNIX operating
	system, then an appropriate error diagnostic is displayed,
	upon the use of this command.

	Full NQS manager privileges are required to use this command.

#HELP_SETPERPROMEM

	The command:

	  SEt PER_Process Memory_limit = (<memory-limit>) <batch-queue-name>

	is used to set the PER-PROCESS maximum memory usage limit for
	any batch request subsequently placed in the named batch queue,
	when the request does not otherwise define an explicit PER-
	PROCESS maximum memory usage limit (as chosen by the origina-
	ting user).

	Each batch request when placed in a batch queue, is examined
	to ensure that any user-specified memory usage limit for the
	request does not exceed the memory usage limit (if configured),
	for the containing batch queue.

	If an attempt is made to queue a batch request which asks for
	a larger maximum memory usage limit than the destination batch
	queue has defined as allowable, then the request is rejected.

	The maximum memory usage limit assigned to a particular batch
	request is determined at the time that the request is queued.
	If the memory usage limit for the containing batch queue is later
	reduced, no requests in the queue will be affected.  However,
	if the new memory usage limit value is less than the memory
	usage limit requested by a previously queued request, then a
	warning diagnostic is displayed.

	The memory consumed by ANY process comprising the running batch
	request cannot exceed the PER-PROCESS maximum determined when
	the request was queued.

	A <memory-limit> is specified as a single integer less than
	100,000,000 with an optional fractional part, possibly followed
	by one of the case insensitive unit names of:

		Unit name:	Meaning:

		 b		 bytes
		 w		 words
		 kb		 2 to the 10th bytes
		 kw		 2 to the 10th words
		 mb		 2 to the 20th bytes
		 mw		 2 to the 20th words
		 gb		 2 to the 30th bytes
		 gw		 2 to the 30th words


	Example valid <memory-limit> specifications are therefore:

		200000		(specifies 200,000 bytes)
		200000B		(specifies 200,000 bytes)
		200 Kw		(specifies 204,800 words)
		.2 MW		(specifies 209,715 words)

	Internally, qmgr converts the specified value into bytes
	or words, whichever is more natural for your machine.
	If the resulting value cannot be represented in a signed
	long integer, then qmgr gives you the largest value that
	it can.
	
	Depending on the underlying UNIX implementation upon which NQS
	is running, this limit may, or may not be configurable.  If
	memory usage limits are not supported by the host UNIX operating
	system, then an appropriate error diagnostic is displayed, upon
	the use of this command.

	Full NQS manager privileges are required to use this command.

#HELP_SETPERPROPER

	The command:

	  SEt PER_Process Permfile_limit=(<permfile-limit>) <batch-queue-name>

	is used to set the PER-PROCESS permanent-file size limit for any
	batch request subsequently placed in the named batch queue, when
	the request does not otherwise define an explicit PER-PROCESS
	permanent-file size usage limit (as chosen by the originating
	user).

	Each batch request when placed in a batch queue, is examined
	to ensure that any user-specified permanent-file size limit for
	the request does not exceed the permanent-file size limit (if
	configured), for the containing batch queue.

	If an attempt is made to queue a batch request which asks for
	a larger permanent-file size limit than the destination batch
	queue has defined as allowable, then the request is rejected.

	The permanent-file size limit assigned to a particular batch
	request is determined at the time that the request is queued.
	If the permanent-file size limit for the containing batch queue
	is later reduced, no requests in the queue will be affected.
	However, if the new permanent-file size limit value is less
	than the permanent-file size usage limit requested by a
	previously queued request, then a warning diagnostic is displayed.

	The size of any permanent-file created by ANY process comprising
	the running batch request cannot exceed the PER-PROCESS maximum
	permanent file size as determined when the request was queued.

	A <permfile-limit> is specified as a single integer less than
	100,000,000 with an optional fractional part, possibly followed
	by one of the case insensitive unit names of:

		Unit name:	Meaning:

		 b		 bytes
		 w		 words
		 kb		 2 to the 10th bytes
		 kw		 2 to the 10th words
		 mb		 2 to the 20th bytes
		 mw		 2 to the 20th words
		 gb		 2 to the 30th bytes
		 gw		 2 to the 30th words


	Example valid <permfile-limit> specifications are therefore:

		200000		(specifies 200,000 bytes)
		200000B		(specifies 200,000 bytes)
		200 Kw		(specifies 204,800 words)
		.2 MW		(specifies 209,715 words)

	Internally, qmgr converts the specified value into bytes
	or words, whichever is more natural for your machine.
	If the resulting value cannot be represented in a signed
	long integer, then qmgr gives you the largest value that
	it can.
	
	Depending on the underlying UNIX implementation upon which NQS
	is running, this limit may, or may not be configurable.  If
	permanent-file size limits are not supported by the host UNIX
	operating system, then an appropriate error diagnostic is
	displayed, upon the use of this command.

	Full NQS manager privileges are required to use this command.

	See also:

	  SEt PER_Request Tempfile_limit <parameters>
	  SEt PER_Process Tempfile_limit <parameters>
	  SEt PER_Request Permfile_limit <parameters>

#HELP_SETPERPROTEM

	The command:

	  SEt PER_Process Tempfile_limit=(<tempfile-limit>) <batch-queue-name>

	is used to set the PER-PROCESS temporary-file size limit for any
	batch request subsequently placed in the named batch queue, when
	the request does not otherwise define an explicit PER-PROCESS
	temporary-file size usage limit (as chosen by the originating
	user).

	Each batch request when placed in a batch queue, is examined to
