	configured), for the containing batch queue.

	If an attempt is made to queue a batch request which asks for a
	larger temporary-file size limit than the destination batch queue
	has defined as allowable, then the request is rejected.

	The temporary-file size limit assigned to a particular batch
	request is determined at the time that the request is queued.
	If the temporary-file size limit for the containing batch queue
	is later reduced, no requests in the queue will be affected.
	However, if the new temporary-file size limit value is less than
	the temporary-file size usage limit requested by a previously
	queued request, then a warning diagnostic is displayed.

	The size of any temporary-file created by ANY process comprising
	the running batch request cannot exceed the PER-PROCESS maximum
	temporary file size as determined when the request was queued.

	A <tempfile-limit> is specified as a single integer less than
	100,000,000 with an optional fractional part, possibly followed
	by one of the case insensitive unit names of:

		Unit name:	Meaning:

		 b		 bytes
		 w		 words
		 kb		 2 to the 10th bytes
		 kw		 2 to the 10th words
		 mb		 2 to the 20th bytes
		 mw		 2 to the 20th words
		 gb		 2 to the 30th bytes
		 gw		 2 to the 30th words


	Example valid <tempfile-limit> specifications are therefore:

		200000		(specifies 200,000 bytes)
		200000B		(specifies 200,000 bytes)
		200 Kw		(specifies 204,800 words)
		.2 MW		(specifies 209,715 words)

	Internally, qmgr converts the specified value into bytes
	or words, whichever is more natural for your machine.
	If the resulting value cannot be represented in a signed
	long integer, then qmgr gives you the largest value that
	it can.
	
	Depending on the underlying UNIX implementation upon which NQS
	is running, this limit may, or may not be configurable.  If
	temporary-file size limits are not supported by the host UNIX
	operating system, then an appropriate error diagnostic is
	displayed, upon the use of this command.

	Full NQS manager privileges are required to use this command.

	See also:

	  SEt PER_Request Permfile_limit <parameters>
	  SEt PER_Process Permfile_limit <parameters>
	  SEt PER_Request Tempfile_limit <parameters>

#HELP_SETPERREQ

	The commands:

	  SEt PER_Request Cpu_limit <parameters>
	  SEt PER_Request Memory_limit <parameters>
	  SEt PER_Request Ncpus <parameters>
	  SEt PER_Request Permfile_limit <parameters>
	  SEt PER_Request Tempfile_limit <parameters>

	are used to set the PER-REQUEST maximum CPU-time limit, maximum
	number of cpus (nodes), memory consumption limit, maximum 
	permanent-file space limit, and maximum temporary-file space 
	limit, for all batch requests subsequently placed in the named 
	batch queue, in the absence of an explicit corresponding 
	user-specified quota limit.

	Each batch request when placed in a batch queue, is examined
	to ensure that all of the user-specified quota limits for the 
	request do not exceed the corresponding maximum limit for the
	containing batch queue (provided that the corresponding queue
	limit has been configured).

	If an attempt is made to queue a batch request which asks for
	more of a particular resource than the destination batch queue
	has defined as allowable, then the request is rejected.

	All possible limits that can be applied to a particular batch
	request are resolved at the time that the request is queued.
	If a particular batch queue limit is later reduced, no requests
	in the queue will be affected.  However, if the new limit value
	is less than the corresponding limit requested by a previously
	queued request, then a warning diagnostic is displayed.

	Depending on the underlying UNIX implementation upon which NQS
	is running, some, all, or none of these limits may be configurable.
	If the named quota limit is not supported by the underlying
	operating system, then an appropriate error diagnostic is
	displayed, if an attempt is made to set the unsupported limit.
	
	More information is available:

	  Help SEt PER_Request Cpu_limit
	  Help SEt PER_Request Memory_limit
	  Help SEt PER_Request Ncpus
	  Help SEt PER_Request Permfile_limit
	  Help SEt PER_Request Tempfile_limit

	Full NQS manager privileges are required to use this command.

#HELP_SETPERREQCPUT

	The command:

	  SEt PER_Request Cpu_limit = (<cpu-time-limit>) <batch-queue-name>

	is used to set the PER-REQUEST maximum CPU-time limit for any
	batch request subsequently placed in the named batch queue, when
	the request does not otherwise define an explicit PER-REQUEST
	maximum CPU-time limit (as chosen by the originating user).

	Each batch request when placed in a batch queue, is examined to
	ensure that any user-specified CPU-time limit for the request
	does not exceed the CPU-time limit (if configured), for the
	containing batch queue.

	If an attempt is made to queue batch request which asks for
	more CPU-time than the destination batch queue has defined as
	allowable, then the request is rejected.

	The maximum CPU-time to be given to a particular batch request
	at execution time is determined at the time that the request is
	queued.  If the CPU-time limit for the containing batch queue
	is later reduced, no requests in the queue will be affected.
	However, if the new CPU-time limit value is less than the
	CPU-time limit requested by a previously queued request,
	then a warning diagnostic is displayed.

	The sum of the CPU-time consumed by each process comprising the
	running batch request cannot exceed the PER-REQUEST maximum
	determined when the request was queued.

	A <cpu-time-limit> may be specified as seconds, minutes and
	seconds, or hours, minutes, and seconds. Milliseconds may
	be included at the setter's option. Milliseconds may also
	appear alone. Here are the accepted formats:

		hh:mm:ss.ms	hh:mm:ss
		mm:ss.ms	mm:ss
		ss.ms		ss
		.ms
	
	Example valid <cpu-time-limit> specifications are therefore:

		2010		(specifies 2,010 seconds)
		2010.234	(specifies 2,010 seconds and 234
				 milliseconds)
		59:23.21	(specifies 59 minutes, 23 seconds,
				 and 210 milliseconds)
		99912:0:0	(specifies 99,912 hours, 0 minutes,
				 0 seconds, and 0 milliseconds)


	Depending on the underlying UNIX implementation upon which NQS
	is running, this limit may, or may not be configurable.  If
	CPU-time limits are not supported by the host UNIX operating
	system, then an appropriate error diagnostic is displayed,
	upon the use of this command.

	Full NQS manager privileges are required to use this command.

#HELP_SETPERREQMEM

	The command:

	  SEt PER_Request Memory_limit = (<memory-limit>) <batch-queue-name>

	is used to set the PER-REQUEST maximum memory usage limit for
	any batch request subsequently placed in the named batch queue,
	when the request does not otherwise define an explicit PER-
	REQUEST maximum memory usage limit (as chosen by the origina-
	ting user).

	Each batch request when placed in a batch queue, is examined to
	ensure that any user-specified memory usage limit for the request
	does not exceed the memory usage limit (if configured), for the
	containing batch queue.

	If an attempt is made to queue a batch request which asks for
	a larger maximum memory usage limit than the destination batch
	queue has defined as allowable, then the request is rejected.

	The maximum memory usage limit assigned to a particular batch
	request is determined at the time that the request is queued.
	If the memory usage limit for the containing batch queue is later
	reduced, no requests in the queue will be affected.  However,
	if the new memory usage limit value is less than the memory usage
	limit requested by a previously queued request, then a warning
	diagnostic is displayed.

	The sum of the memory sizes of each process comprising the
	running batch request cannot exceed the PER-REQUEST maximum
	determined when the request was queued.

	A <memory-limit> is specified as a single integer less than
	100,000,000 with an optional fractional part, possibly followed
	by one of the case insensitive unit names of:

		Unit name:	Meaning:

		 b		 bytes
		 w		 words
		 kb		 2 to the 10th bytes
		 kw		 2 to the 10th words
		 mb		 2 to the 20th bytes
		 mw		 2 to the 20th words
		 gb		 2 to the 30th bytes
		 gw		 2 to the 30th words


	Example valid <memory-limit> specifications are therefore:

		200000		(specifies 200,000 bytes)
		200000B		(specifies 200,000 bytes)
		200 Kw		(specifies 204,800 words)
		.2 MW		(specifies 209,715 words)

	Internally, qmgr converts the specified value into bytes
	or words, whichever is more natural for your machine.
	If the resulting value cannot be represented in a signed
	long integer, then qmgr gives you the largest value that
	it can.
	
	Depending on the underlying UNIX implementation upon which NQS
	is running, this limit may, or may not be configurable.  If
	memory usage limits are not supported by the host UNIX operating
	system, then an appropriate error diagnostic is displayed, upon
	the use of this command.

	Full NQS manager privileges are required to use this command.

#HELP_SETPERREQNCPUS

	The command:

	  SEt PER_Request Ncpu = <ncpu> <batch-queue-name>

	is used to set the PER-REQUEST number of cpus (nodes) limit for
	any batch request subsequently placed in the named batch queue.

	If the Ncpus limit for the containing batch queue is later
	reduced, no requests in the queue will be affected.  However,
	if the new ncpus usage limit value is less than the ncpus usage
	limit requested by a previously queued request, then a warning
	diagnostic is displayed.

	The sum of the cpus used by each process comprising the
	running batch request cannot exceed the PER-REQUEST maximum
	determined when the request was queued.

#HELP_SETPERREQPER

	The command:

	  SEt PER_Request Permfile_limit=(<permfile-limit>) <batch-queue-name>

	is used to set the PER-REQUEST permanent-file space usage
	limit for any batch request subsequently placed in the named
	batch queue, when the request does not otherwise define an
	explicit PER-REQUEST permanent-file space usage limit (as
	chosen by the originating user).

	Each request when placed in a batch queue, is examined to ensure
	that any user-specified permanent-file space usage limit for the
	request does not exceed the permanent-file space usage limit (if
	configured), for the containing batch queue.

	If an attempt is made to queue a batch request which asks for
	a larger permanent-file space usage limit than the destination
	batch queue has defined as allowable, then the request is
	rejected.

	The permanent-file space usage limit assigned to a particular
	batch request is determined at the time that the request is
	queued.  If the permanent-file space usage limit for the
	containing batch queue is later reduced, no requests in the
	queue will be affected.  However, if the new permanent-file
	space usage limit value is less than the permanent-file space
	usage limit requested by a previously queued request, then a
	warning diagnostic is displayed.

	The sizes of all temporary files created by any process comprising
	the running batch request, when summed together, cannot exceed the
	PER-REQUEST maximum determined when the request was queued.

	A <permfile-limit> is specified as a single integer less than
	100,000,000 with an optional fractional part, possibly followed
	by one of the case insensitive unit names of:

		Unit name:	Meaning:

		 b		 bytes
		 w		 words
		 kb		 2 to the 10th bytes
		 kw		 2 to the 10th words
		 mb		 2 to the 20th bytes
		 mw		 2 to the 20th words
		 gb		 2 to the 30th bytes
		 gw		 2 to the 30th words


	Example valid <permfile-limit> specifications are therefore:

		200000		(specifies 200,000 bytes)
		200000B		(specifies 200,000 bytes)
		200 Kw		(specifies 204,800 words)
		.2 MW		(specifies 209,715 words)

	Internally, qmgr converts the specified value into bytes
	or words, whichever is more natural for your machine.
	If the resulting value cannot be represented in a signed
	long integer, then qmgr gives you the largest value that
	it can.
	
	Depending on the underlying UNIX implementation upon which NQS
	is running, this limit may, or may not be configurable.  If
	permanent-file space limits are not supported by the host UNIX
	operating system, then an appropriate error diagnostic is
	displayed, upon the use of this command.

	Full NQS manager privileges are required to use this command.

	See also:

	  SEt PER_Request Tempfile_limit <parameters>
	  SEt PER_Process Tempfile_limit <parameters>
	  SEt PER_Process Permfile_limit <parameters>

#HELP_SETPERREQTEM

	The command:

	  SEt PER_Request Tempfile_limit=(<tempfile-limit>) <batch-queue-name>

	is used to set the PER-REQUEST temporary-file space usage
	limit for any batch request subsequently placed in the named
	batch queue, when the request does not otherwise define an
	explicit PER-REQUEST temporary-file space usage limit (as
	chosen by the originating user).

	Each batch request when placed in a batch queue, is examined
	to ensure that any user-specified temporary-file space usage
	limit for the request does not exceed the temporary-file space
	usage limit (if configured), for the containing batch queue.

	If an attempt is made to queue a batch request which asks for
	a larger temporary-file space usage limit than the destination
	batch queue has defined as allowable, then the request is
	rejected.

	The temporary-file space usage limit assigned to a particular
	batch request is determined at the time that the request is
	queued.  If the temporary-file space usage limit for the
	containing batch queue is later reduced, no requests in the
	queue will be affected.  However, if the new temporary-file
	space usage limit value is less than the temporary-file space
	usage limit requested by a previously queued request, then a
	warning diagnostic is displayed.

	The sizes of all temporary-files created by any process comprising
	the batch running request, when summed together, cannot exceed
	the PER-REQUEST maximum determined when the request was queued.

	A <tempfile-limit> is specified as a single integer less than
	100,000,000 with an optional fractional part, possibly followed
	by one of the case insensitive unit names of:

		Unit name:	Meaning:

		 b		 bytes
		 w		 words
		 kb		 2 to the 10th bytes
		 kw		 2 to the 10th words
		 mb		 2 to the 20th bytes
		 mw		 2 to the 20th words
		 gb		 2 to the 30th bytes
		 gw		 2 to the 30th words


	Example valid <tempfile-limit> specifications are therefore:

		200000		(specifies 200,000 bytes)
		200000B		(specifies 200,000 bytes)
		200 Kw		(specifies 204,800 words)
		.2 MW		(specifies 209,715 words)

	Internally, qmgr converts the specified value into bytes
	or words, whichever is more natural for your machine.
	If the resulting value cannot be represented in a signed
	long integer, then qmgr gives you the largest value that
	it can.
	
	Depending on the underlying UNIX implementation upon which NQS
	is running, this limit may, or may not be configurable.  If
	temporary-file space limits are not supported by the host UNIX
	operating system, then an appropriate error diagnostic is
	displayed, upon the use of this command.

	Full NQS manager privileges are required to use this command.

	See also:

		SEt PER_Request Permfile_limit <parameters>
		SEt PER_Process Permfile_limit <parameters>
		SEt PER_Process Tempfile_limit <parameters>

#HELP_SETPIPCLI

	The command:

	  SEt PIpe_client = (<client-and-args>) <queue-name>

	allows the client associated with the named pipe queue to be
	changed if necessary.

	The text placed within the parentheses defines the fully
	qualified pathname of the client, and client arguments that are
	to be used as the command line for a pipe queue client
	invocation by NQS.

	The named queue cannot refer to a batch or device queue.

	Full NQS manager privileges are required to use this command.

#HELP_SETPRI

	The command:

	  SEt PRiority = <priority-level> <queue-name>

	is used to change the inter-queue priority of an NQS queue.  The
	queue named as a parameter of the command must already exist.

	The priority value assigned to the queue must be in the range:
	[0..63].

	Full NQS manager privileges are required to use this command.

#HELP_SETRUNLIM

	The command:

	  SEt Run_limit = <run-limit> <queue-name>

	is used to change the run-limit of an NQS batch or pipe queue.
	The queue named as a parameter of the command must already exist.

	The run-limit for a batch or pipe queue determines the maximum
	number of requests that will be allowed to run in the queue at any
	given time.

	A run-limit cannot be applied to a device queue.

	Operator privileges are required to use this command.

#HELP_SETQUEDLIM
 
        The command:
 
          SEt Queue_request_limit = (<queue-request-limit>) <queue-name>
 
        is used to change the maximum number of requests that a single
        user can have at one time (in any state) in the specified NQS
        batch queue.  Any attempt to submit additional requests above this
        limit will be rejected with the  error "Permission denied".
        The <queue-request-limit> must be an integer greater than zero
        and less than 32768 or the word "unlimited".  Any word beginning 
        with 'u' will be accepted as a synonym for "unlimited".
 
        The <queue-name> must be a batch queue that already exists.  
 
        Full NQS manager privileges are required to use this command.
 
#HELP_SETSHS

	The commands:

	  SEt SHell_strategy FIxed <parameters>
	  SEt SHell_strategy FRee
	  SEt SHell_strategy Login

	are used to respectively set a fixed, free, or login shell
	strategy, for the execution of batch requests.

	More information is available:

	  Help SEt SHell_strategy FIxed <parameters>
	  Help SEt SHell_strategy FRee
	  Help SEt SHell_strategy Login

#HELP_SETSHSFIX

	The command:
	
	  SEt SHell_strategy FIxed = (<shell-pathname>)

	is used to set a "fixed shell strategy", where the pathname of
	<shell-pathname> identifies the shell that will be used to
	execute ALL batch requests.

	This command exists for NQS installations that use only
	one type of shell (e.g. Bourne, C-shell, etc.).

	Full NQS manager privileges are required to use this command.

	See also:

	  SEt SHell_strategy FRee
	  SEt SHell_strategy Login

#HELP_SETSHSFRE

	The command:

	  SEt SHell_strategy FRee

	is used to set a "free shell strategy".  This means that the
	user's login shell will be spawned by NQS when a given user's
	batch request is run, with the feature that the user's login
	shell will then examine the user's batch request shell script,
	to determine the type of shell (e.g. C-shell, Bourne shell,
	etc.), to be used to execute the batch request shell script.

	This will result in an extra shell being spawned for all batch
	request executions, EXACTLY as would happen if the user were
	running their batch request script interactively.

	Full NQS manager privileges are required to use this command.

	See also:

	  SEt SHell_strategy FIxed <parameters>
	  SEt SHell_strategy Login

#HELP_SETSHSLOG

	The command:
	
	  SEt SHell_strategy Login

	is used to set a "login shell strategy."  This means that the
	user's login shell will be spawned to run ALL batch request
	scripts, without examining the batch shell script to determine
	which type of script it is.

	Full NQS manager privileges are required to use this command.

	See also:

	  SEt SHell_strategy FIxed <parameters>
	  SEt SHell_strategy FRee

#HELP_SETSTA

	The command:

	  SEt STack_limit = (<stack-limit>) <queue-name>

	is used to set the per-process maximum stack-segment size limit
	for any request subsequently placed in the named queue, when
	the request does not otherwise define an explicit per-process
	maximum stack-segment size limit (as chosen by the originating
	user).

	Each request, when queued, is examined to ensure that any user-
	specified stack-segment size limit for the request does not
	exceed the stack-segment size limit (if configured), for the
	containing queue.

	If an attempt is made to queue a request which asks for a larger
	stack-segment size limit than the destination queue has defined
	as allowable, then the request is rejected.

	The maximum stack-segment size limit assigned to a particular
	request is determined at the time that the request is queued.
	If the stack-segment size limit for the containing queue is
	later reduced, no requests in the queue will be affected.
	However, if the new stack-segment size limit value is less than
	the stack-segment size limit requested by a previously queued
	request, then a warning diagnostic is displayed.

	The stack-segment size of ANY process comprising the running
	request cannot exceed the maximum in force when the request
	was queued.

	A <stack-limit> is specified as a single integer less
	than 100,000,000 with an optional fractional part, possibly
	followed by one of the case insensitive unit names of:

		Unit name:	Meaning:

		 b		 bytes
		 w		 words
		 kb		 2 to the 10th bytes
		 kw		 2 to the 10th words
		 mb		 2 to the 20th bytes
		 mw		 2 to the 20th words
		 gb		 2 to the 30th bytes
		 gw		 2 to the 30th words


	Example valid <stack-limit> specifications are therefore:

		200000		(specifies 200,000 bytes)
		200000B		(specifies 200,000 bytes)
		200 Kw		(specifies 204,800 words)
		.2 MW		(specifies 209,715 words)

	Internally, qmgr converts the specified value into bytes
	or words, whichever is more natural for your machine.
	If the resulting value cannot be represented in a signed
	long integer, then qmgr gives you the largest value that
	it can.
	
	Depending on the underlying UNIX implementation upon which NQS
	is running, this limit may, or may not be configurable.  If
	stack-segment size limits are not supported by the host UNIX
	operating system, then an appropriate error diagnostic is
	displayed, upon the use of this command.

	Stack-segment limits cannot be applied to pipe queues.  Any
	attempt to set a stack-segment limit for a pipe queue will fail,
	with an appropriate error diagnostic being displayed.
	
	Full NQS manager privileges are required to use this command.

#HELP_SETUNRACC

	The command:

	  SEt Unrestricted_access <queue-name>

	is used to indicate that all users are to have access to
	the named queue.  In order to enforce access restrictions
	on a queue where access is currently unrestricted, see
	Help SEt NO_Access.
	
	In order for a user to have access to a queue, it is only
	necessary for ONE of the following to be true:
	
	1) Queue access is unlimited.
	2) His or her login group has access. (Help ADd Groups)
	3) His or her account has access. (Help ADd Users)

	Full NQS manager privileges are required to use this command.

#HELP_SETWOR

	The command:

	  SEt Working_set_limit = (<working-set-limit>) <queue-name>

	is used to set the per-process working-set limit (physical memory
	quota) for any request subsequently placed in the named queue,
	when the request does not otherwise define an explicit per-process
	working-set limit (as chosen by the originating user).

	Each request, when queued, is examined to ensure that any user-
	specified working-set limit for the request does not exceed the
	working-set limit (if configured), for the containing queue.

	If an attempt is made to queue a request which asks for a larger
	working-set limit than the destination queue has defined as
	allowable, then the request is rejected.

	The working-set limit assigned to a particular request is
	determined at the time that the request is queued.  If the
	working-set limit for the containing queue is later reduced,
	no requests in the queue will be affected.  However, if the new
	working-set limit is less than the working-set limit requested
	by a previously queued request, then a warning diagnostic is
	displayed.

	When physical memory becomes scarce on the supporting machine,
	the system will prefer to take away physical memory from those
	processes exceeding their working-set limit.

	A <working-set-limit> is specified as a single integer less
	than 100,000,000 with an optional fractional part, possibly
	followed by one of the case insensitive unit names of:

		Unit name:	Meaning:

		 b		 bytes
		 w		 words
		 kb		 2 to the 10th bytes
		 kw		 2 to the 10th words
		 mb		 2 to the 20th bytes
		 mw		 2 to the 20th words
		 gb		 2 to the 30th bytes
		 gw		 2 to the 30th words


	Example valid <working-set-limit> specifications are therefore:

		200000		(specifies 200,000 bytes)
		200000B		(specifies 200,000 bytes)
		200 Kw		(specifies 204,800 words)
		.2 MW		(specifies 209,715 words)

	Internally, qmgr converts the specified value into bytes
	or words, whichever is more natural for your machine.
	If the resulting value cannot be represented in a signed
	long integer, then qmgr gives you the largest value that
	it can.
	
	Depending on the underlying UNIX implementation upon which NQS
	is running, this limit may, or may not be configurable.  If
	working-set limits are not supported by the host UNIX operating
	system, then an appropriate error diagnostic is displayed, upon
	the use of this command.

	Working-set-limits cannot be applied to pipe queues.  Any attempt
	to set a working-set-limit for a pipe queue will fail, with an
	appropriate error diagnostic being displayed.
	
	Full NQS manager privileges are required to use this command.

#HELP_SHO

	The commands:

	  SHOw All
	  SHOw Device <parameters>
	  SHOw Forms
	  SHOw LImits_supported
	  SHOw LOng Queue <parameters>
	  SHOw Managers
	  SHOw Parameters
	  SHOw Queue <parameters>

	are used to display NQS status information.

	More information is available:

	  Help SHOw All
	  Help SHOw Device
	  Help SHOw Forms
	  Help SHOw LImits_supported
	  Help SHOw LOng Queue
	  Help SHOw Managers
	  Help SHOw Parameters
	  Help SHOw Queue

#HELP_SHOALL

	The command:

	  SHOw All

	displays general status information on all of the NQS devices,
	forms, limits supported, managers, queues, and the general
	operating parameters.

#HELP_SHODEV

	The command:

	  SHOw Device [ <device-name> ]

	is used to respectively obtain general status information on all
	of the NQS devices, or more detailed status information concerning
	a specific device.

	If no <device-name> is specified, then general status information
	is displayed for all of the NQS devices.

	If a <device-name> is specified, then status information concerning
	the named device is displayed including any queue to device
	mappings for the named device.

#HELP_SHOFOR

	The command:

	  SHOw Forms

	is used to display the forms included in the device forms list.

#HELP_SHOLIM

	The command:

	  SHOw LImits_supported

	is used to display the set of request and process limits enforceable
	by the host UNIX operating system.  Each of the limits shown here
	can be configured for individual batch queues from within qmgr.

	For a discussion of the individual limit commands, more information
	is available:

	  Help SEt Corefile_limit <parameters>
	  Help SEt DAta_limit <parameters>
	  Help SEt NIce_value <parameters>
	  Help SEt PER_Process Cpu_limit <parameters>
	  Help SEt PER_Process Memory_limit <parameters>
	  Help SEt PER_Process Permfile_limit <parameters>
	  Help SEt PER_Process Tempfile_limit <parameters>
	  Help SEt PER_Request Cpu_limit <parameters>
	  Help SEt PER_Request Memory_limit <parameters>
	  Help SEt PER_Request Permfile_limit <parameters>
	  Help SEt PER_Request Tempfile_limit <parameters>
	  Help SEt STack_limit <parameters>
	  Help SEt Working_set_limit <parameters>
	
#HELP_SHOLONGQUE

	The command:

	  SHOw LOng Queue [ <queue-name> [ <user-name> ] ]

	is used to display status information about NQS queues in
	a long format.

	If no queue name is specified, then status information is
	displayed for all NQS queues.

	If a queue name is specified, then specific status information
	for the named queue is displayed, including the ordering of requests 
	within the queue. If a user name is specified, then the requests
	shown will be limited to those belonging to that user.

#HELP_SHOMAN

	The command:

	  SHOw Managers

	is used to display the accounts included in the NQS manager account
	access list.

#HELP_SHOPAR

	The command:

	  SHOw Parameters

	is used to display the current values for the general NQS
	operating parameters

#HELP_SHOQUE

	The command:

	  SHOw Queue [ <queue-name> [ <user-name> ] ]

	is used to display status information about NQS queues in
	a short format.

	If no queue name is specified, then status information is
	displayed for all NQS queues.

	If a queue name is specified, then specific status information
	for the named queue is displayed, including the ordering of requests 
	within the queue. If a user name is specified, then the requests
	shown will be limited to those belonging to that user.

#HELP_SHUTDOWN

	The command:

	  SHUtdown [ <grace-period> ]

	is used to shutdown the Network Queueing System on the local
	supporting host.

	For each queue that is running, the semantic equivalent of the
	ABort Queue command is performed, with the exception that all requests 
	terminated as a result of the shutdown command, are requeued for
	later execution.

	The <grace-period> parameter is optional, and if present, specifies
	the number of seconds in real time to wait before sending a
	SIGKILL signal to all of the remaining processes for each remain-
	ing request still running at the end of the grace-period.

	If <grace-period> is not specified, then a default value of
	twenty (20) seconds is used.

	Operator privileges are required to use this command.

#HELP_STAQUE

	The command:

	  STArt Queue <queue-name>

	is used to start the named queue.  If the current queue is already
	started, then no action occurs.  Otherwise, the queue is started,
	and requests in the named queue become eligible for execution.

	Operator privileges are required to use this command.

#HELP_STOQUE

	The command:

	  STOp Queue <queue-name>

	is used to stop the named queue.  Any requests in the queue that
	are currently running are allowed to complete execution.  All other
	requests eligible for execution in the queue are prevented from
	running.

	A queue which has been stopped, can still accept new requests.
	However, no requests will be run which reside within the named
	queue until the queue has been explicitly started again by the:

	  STArt Queue <queue-name>

	command.

	Operator privileges are required to use this command.

#HELP_UNLDAE

	The command:

	  Unlock Local_daemon

	is used to remove a lock that has been keeping the NQS local
	daemon in memory.  See plock(2).

	Operator privileges are required to use this command.
